\chapter{Tutorial: Screen a search in tiers}
\label{ch:tutorial-tiered-search}

[[tiered-search]] follows the export tutorial
(\cref{ch:tutorial-enrich-verify-export}) and takes up a problem the
earlier lessons hide by keeping their searches small: a real search
returns more records than anyone screens one by one.
Scholar's answer is to screen the head of the answers first (a
\emph{tier}) and to estimate what the rest holds from a seeded random
sample of it.
The object of learning is that answer and its limits.
Four aspects are critical, and each step below opens one of them:
\begin{description}
\item[A place belongs to an answer] A tier selected by place is the
  union of the heads of several answers, so two wordings of one
  question give a tier of at most twice the papers of one, and fewer
  when their heads share papers (the same work, or a published paper
  and its preprint merged into one record).
\item[Only a ranked answer has a head] A place counts only in an
  answer ordered by relevance; being first in an answer in another
  order, or in no known order, does not select a record.
\item[A tier is computed from the pending papers] The tier and the
  rest are recomputed from the papers still pending each time.
  Deciding the tier empties the tier's count and leaves the rest as it
  was; only papers outside the tier decided before the draw change
  what the draw can pick.
\item[The rest is estimated, not screened] A sample of the rest gives
  a share, with an interval unless the sample took the whole rest, and
  nothing at all until it is screened; the report's sentence says so in
  Scholar's own words.
\end{description}

The arc keeps one session, [[tiers]], and one rule, [[--top 3]],
invariant throughout:
\begin{enumerate}
\item search one wording and take its tier (steps~1--2),
\item search a second wording into the session and read what the
  session records about each answer (steps~3--4),
\item predict, then see, how many papers the same tier takes from two
  answers (steps~5--6),
\item apply the rule to an answer in another order (step~7),
\item screen the tier (step~8),
\item draw a sample of the rest and read the report before and after
  screening it (steps~9--11),
\item say what a method section can claim from all this (step~12).
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{What the keyless providers allow.}
The lesson uses OpenAlex only, since the tutorial shell keeps its
rate-limit memory in the sandbox and a lesson must run without keys
(\cref{ch:tutorial-first-search}).
That costs one contrast.
OpenAlex, arXiv and HAL record their answers as ordered by relevance
(each provider's [[ANSWER_ORDER]], \cref{sec:answer-orders}).
Semantic Scholar, whose key is optional, records [[relevance]] for
plain words with a limit, [[citations]] for a limited query with
operators (its bulk search, most cited first) and [[unknown]] without a
limit ([[s2_order]], \cref{sec:s2-two-searches}); but its anonymous
requests draw on one pool shared by every client without a key
(\cref{sec:semantic-scholar}), so a lesson step on it could be refused.
DBLP records [[unknown]] and answers scripts with an anti-bot page
(\cref{sec:dblp-transient-failures}).
The learner can therefore not be sure to \emph{see} an answer in
another order beside a ranked one.
Step~7 builds that contrast as a question instead, after step~6 has
shown the tier's own count of papers \enquote{placed only in answers
not ordered by relevance}, so the question asks the learner to apply a
line they have read rather than to guess.

\section{Tutorial source}

The whole tutorial is one tangled root chunk, with each step in its own
named sub-chunk, as in the other lessons.

<<[[tiered-search.md]]>>=
---
id: tiered-search
title: Screen a search in tiers
summary: Search one question in two wordings, screen the first tier of the answers, and estimate the rest from a seeded sample.
---

<<first wording step>>

<<tier of one answer step>>

<<second wording step>>

<<show the answers step>>

<<predict the tier question step>>

<<export the tier step>>

<<other order question step>>

<<decide the tier step>>

<<draw the rest step>>

<<report before screening step>>

<<screen the sample step>>

