@

\section{How the steps recognise a command}

Every step that runs a command checks the command typed with a regular
expression, and pytorial matches it against the terminal's record of
the step with [[re.search]], without [[re.DOTALL]]
(\cref{sec:tutorial-command}).
The patterns of this lesson follow four rules, so that every spelling
the command line accepts passes and a near miss does not:
\begin{description}
\item[Names end at white space] A session name is followed by
  [[(?=\s|$)]], not by [[\b]], which would also match before the
  hyphen of [[tiers-old]]; the name may stand in quotes, as the shell
  takes [[-n "tiers"]] and [[-n 'tiers']] for [[-n tiers]].
\item[Long options take a space or an equals sign] [[--limit=5]],
  [[--name=tiers]], [[--rest-sample=2]] are all accepted, by
  \verb|[\s=]+| after the option; short options may be glued to their
  value ([[-l5]], [[-fscreen-sheet]]), as Click allows.
\item[A command may continue on the next line] A lookahead reads
  [[(?:.|\\\n)*]] rather than [[.*]], so a command typed over two lines,
  which the terminal records with a backslash, a line break and the
  continuation prompt, still passes.
\item[Each required value is required] [[--top 3]] and the
  [[--rest-sample 2 --seed 7]] of the draw are part of the pattern, so
  a tier of another size is refused.
\end{description}
A test in the CLI module feeds each search, show, export and draw
pattern spellings it must accept and near misses it must refuse
([[TestTieredSearchPatterns]]).

\section{Steps 1 and 2: One wording and its tier}

A tier is a rule over the answers in a session, and the first critical
aspect is that the rule takes the head of \emph{each} answer.
To make that visible, the lesson applies the rule twice, to one answer
and then to two, and the first application comes right after the first
search.
The search sets what stays invariant for the rest of the lesson: the
provider, the [[--in title-abstract]] filter, the limit and the session
name.
The filter is part of the invariant rather than a variation of its own:
the lesson shows where Scholar records it (step~4), not what it
changes.

The limit of five keeps the practice small and makes the head of each
answer a large part of it, which is what makes the count of step~5
worth predicting.
The required pattern demands the limit (\cref{sec:no-limit-default})
and [[--in title-abstract]], wherever on the line they are typed, and
anchors on Scholar's confirmation line, never on the number of hits.

A search that runs is saved in the session whether or not the step
accepts it: a learner who leaves out [[--in]] is refused by the step,
but the search ran with [[-n tiers]], and its answer now counts in
every tier (a reviewer reproduced a tier of 5 of 11 that way, where the
lesson expects at most 6).
The instructions therefore say how to take such a search out of the
counts: [[sessions retire-search]] with the record's id, which
[[sessions show]] prints in brackets.
A retired search keeps its record and its papers, marked retired; the
tier no longer counts it, so the papers only it returned stay pending
with no place ([[place unknown]] in the tier's counts) and belong to
the rest: the sample of step~9 can draw them, the report's sentence
counts them among the records outside the tier, and the pending count
of step~4 is larger than the two answers hold (a reviewer's run had a
rest of 9 instead of 4).
The instructions say so, and give the clean way out, starting the
lesson over with [[scholar tutorial run --restart tiered-search]]
(a fresh workspace, so a fresh session), or going on knowing that the
rest is larger; no later step states a count that a retired search
would make wrong.
The lesson's later anchors hold with a retired record present: the
listing's heading then reads, for example, \enquote{Queries by
provider: 2 current, 1 retired}, which still holds the anchor of
step~4.

<<first wording step>>=
# Search the first wording

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s(?:-l\s*|--limit[\s=]+)[1-9])(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--in[\s=]+title-abstract(?=\s|$))(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s(?:-n\s*|--name[\s=]+)["']?tiers["']?(?=\s|$))
  - "Saved session:"
hint: "Search OpenAlex with --in title-abstract and -l 5, and save it with -n tiers."
```

Search OpenAlex for one wording of a question, in titles and abstracts
only, and save the answer in a session named `tiers`:

```
scholar search "literate programming" -p openalex --in title-abstract -l 5 -n tiers
```

`--in title-abstract` matches the words in the title and the abstract
only (OpenAlex otherwise also searches the full text it holds). `-l 5`
keeps the practice small: without it, Scholar fetches every result.

A search that runs is saved in the session even when the step does not
accept it, for instance one typed without `--in`. If that happened, take
it out of the counts: `scholar sessions show tiers` prints each search
with an id in brackets, and

```
scholar sessions retire-search tiers --record <id>
```

marks that search retired. The tier no longer counts it, but its
papers stay in the session as pending papers without a place: they
belong to the rest outside the tier, so the sample you draw later can
pick them and the report counts them, and the session holds more
pending papers than the two answers you search for here. The clean way
out is to start the lesson over, which gives it a fresh session:

```
scholar tutorial run --restart tiered-search
```

Or go on, knowing that the rest will be larger.
@

The second step applies the tier option to the one answer the session
holds.
The option is introduced by the instructions with its rule, in the
words of the help text, since the learner cannot be expected to know
it; the count it prints, three of the five pending papers, is the
first value of the comparison that steps~5 and~6 complete.

<<tier of one answer step>>=
# Take the first tier of one answer

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+export\s+["']?tiers["']?(?=\s|$)(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s(?:-f\s*|--format[\s=]+)screen-sheet(?=\s|$))(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--top[\s=]+3(?=\s|$))
  - "Tier:"
hint: "Export the pending papers as a screen sheet with --top 3 and the base name one-answer."
```

The tier option `--top 3` keeps the pending papers at place 3 or better
in an answer ordered by relevance. Apply it to the session's one
answer:

```
scholar sessions export tiers -f screen-sheet --pending --top 3 -o one-answer
```

Look at the `Tier:` line: how many of the pending papers did it select?
@

\section{Steps 3 and 4: A second wording, and what the session records}

The third step repeats the first search with one more word.
The step's hint names the only thing to change, since the learner
should spend no attention on recalling the flags.

<<second wording step>>=
# Search a second wording

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s(?:-l\s*|--limit[\s=]+)[1-9])(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--in[\s=]+title-abstract(?=\s|$))(?=(?:.|\\\n)*documentation)(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s(?:-n\s*|--name[\s=]+)["']?tiers["']?(?=\s|$))
  - "Saved session:"
hint: "The same command as the first search, with the word documentation added to the query."
```

Now search a second wording of the same question into the same
session. Everything but the words stays the same:

```
scholar search "literate programming documentation" -p openalex --in title-abstract -l 5 -n tiers
```

The session now holds two answers of five papers each. (As before, a
search the step did not accept still ran: retire it as in the first
step, or start the lesson over.)
@

[[sessions show]] is where the learner sees the three facts the tier
rests on, each per answer: how many records the answer returned of how
many the database reported ([[hits: 5 of 1,234 (capped at 5)]]), the
order the answer came in ([[order: relevance]]), and the filter that
was applied ([[--in title-abstract]] in the reproduced command).
The \enquote{Screening tier} line below them is Scholar's fixed
overview for planning a screen (the first 100 places, three
databases); it is printed because the answers recorded places.
The instructions point at each of these without saying yet what the
tier will do with two answers, so that the prediction of step~5 has
its premises on the screen and its answer still open.

The anchors are fixed strings of the listing: the heading, the word
[[capped at]] (true whenever an answer had more than five records, which
both wordings will) and the order.

<<show the answers step>>=
# Read what the session records

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+show\s+["']?tiers["']?(?=\s|$)
  - "Queries by provider:"
  - capped at
  - "order: relevance"
hint: "Show the session named tiers."
```

Run `scholar sessions show tiers`.

Look at the lines under `Queries by provider:`. Each search records:

- how many papers it returned of how many the database reported, for
  example `5 of 1,234 (capped at 5)`: the limit cut the answer;
- the order the answer came in, `order: relevance`: the database
  ranked its answer, best match first;
- the command that repeats it, with `--in title-abstract` and
  `--limit 5`.

A search you retired is listed too, marked `(retired ...)`.

Then look at the `Pending` count: how many papers do the two answers
hold together? (A retired search's papers are counted there too.)
@

\section{Step 5: Predict the tier of two answers}

The prediction asks for the size of the same tier over two answers
before the learner sees it.
Its premises are all on the screen: the tier of one answer took three
papers (step~2), the session now holds two answers of five, each
ranked, and the pending count already shows whether the two answers
share papers.
The accepted answer is \enquote{at most 6}.
It has no lower bound worth stating, because the heads can share
papers in two ways: the same work returned by both wordings, and a
published paper and its preprint, which Scholar merges into one record
that keeps the better of the two places (a reviewer reproduced a tier
of 2 of 6 that way).
\enquote{Exactly 6} is the answer of a learner who adds the two heads
without asking whether they share papers, and \enquote{exactly 3} is
the answer of one who thinks of a single ranked list.
\enquote{All the pending papers} is there for the learner who expects a
tier to be a filter on the answers' size rather than on places.

<<predict the tier question step>>=
# Predict the tier of two answers

```tutorial-step
kind: single_select
options:
  - Exactly 3
  - Exactly 6
  - At most 6
  - All the pending papers
answers:
  - At most 6
hint: "--top 3 took three papers from one answer. The session now holds two answers ordered by relevance. Can the two answers' first three places hold the same paper?"
```

`--top 3` took three papers from the session's one answer. The session
now holds two answers of five. How many of its pending papers will
`--top 3` select now?
@

\section{Step 6: The same sheet with and without the tier}

The step is a contrast with one varied aspect: the same export of the
pending papers, once without a tier option and once with [[--top 3]],
everything else invariant.
The step requires both: one pattern takes an export of the pending
papers with no [[--top]] on the line (a negative lookahead), the other
the export with [[--top 3]].
The learner sees the first say \enquote{N paper(s) on the screen sheet}
for every pending paper and the second print the tier's counts:
selected, not selected, how many by each condition, and the papers the
rule could not place.
[[BASE-tier.json]] is the same count as data, written beside the sheet
so that a screen can be described later by the file that selected it;
the learner reads it to see that the rule is stored with its meaning.

The anchors are the commands, Scholar's tier line, and a key of the JSON
file, all fixed text.
The step's last lines connect back to step~5 by asking the learner to
compare the count with the prediction, and say when it comes out below
six, which is the follow-through of the try-first question.

<<export the tier step>>=
# Export the pending papers, with and without a tier

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+export\s+["']?tiers["']?(?=\s|$)(?!(?:.|\\\n)*\s--top)(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s(?:-f\s*|--format[\s=]+)screen-sheet(?=\s|$))(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--pending(?=\s|$))
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+export\s+["']?tiers["']?(?=\s|$)(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s(?:-f\s*|--format[\s=]+)screen-sheet(?=\s|$))(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--top[\s=]+3(?=\s|$))
  - "Tier:"
  - '"not_selected"'
hint: "Two exports of the pending papers as a screen sheet, the first without a tier option, the second with --top 3; then look at tier1-tier.json."
```

Export the pending papers as a screen sheet twice, first all of them,
then only the first tier, and look at what the tier export wrote beside
its sheet:

```
scholar sessions export tiers -f screen-sheet --pending -o everything
scholar sessions export tiers -f screen-sheet --pending --top 3 -o tier1
cat tier1-tier.json
```

The first export lists every pending paper. The second prints
`Tier: N of M pending papers selected` and the counts behind it, and
writes `tier1.tsv` (the sheet to screen) and `tier1-tier.json` (the
same counts, with the rule and what it means).

Compare N with your prediction. N is below six when the two answers
share papers among their first three places: the same paper returned
by both wordings, or a published paper and its preprint, which Scholar
merges into one record that keeps the better place.
@

\section{Step 7: An answer in another order}

This is the contrast the lesson cannot be sure to show (see the
introduction), so it is posed as an application of the line the
learner has just read: \enquote{placed only in answers not ordered by
relevance}.
The question uses what [[scholar syntax]] states about Semantic
Scholar (\enquote{bulk with a limit: most cited first, not ranked}) and
what the code records for it ([[order: citations]], [[s2_order]]), and
names IEEE Xplore's [[order: unknown]] as the second case, since
Scholar asks IEEE for no sort (the IEEE provider's [[ANSWER_ORDER]]).
The command is given in the prose, not as a block to copy, and the
learner is told not to run it.
The options vary only the number; the right one, zero, follows from the
rule as step~2 stated it, and the wrong ones are the answers of a
learner who reads a place as a place whatever the order.

<<other order question step>>=
# An answer in another order

```tutorial-step
kind: single_select
options:
  - "0"
  - "3"
  - "5"
answers:
  - "0"
hint: "Read the last line of the tier counts again: what does it say about answers not ordered by relevance?"
```

Not every answer is ordered by relevance. Semantic Scholar answers a
query with quotes or operators, given a limit, by its bulk search,
most cited first (`scholar syntax` says so), and Scholar records that
answer as `order: citations`; IEEE Xplore's answers it records as
`order: unknown`, since it asks IEEE for no sort.

Suppose a third search, `scholar search '"literate programming" AND
documentation' -p s2 -l 5 -n tiers` (do not run it), returned five
papers that neither OpenAlex search returned. How many of those five
could `--top 3` select?
@

\section{Step 8: Screen the tier}

The tier is screened the way the earlier lessons decide: by a human,
with [[sessions decide --source human]], one command for the keeps and
one for the discards, the papers named by the ids in the sheet's first
column.
The learner reads the sheet with [[cut]], so the step shows that the
sheet is a plain tab-separated file meant for whatever reads it.
A discard needs a tag; [[off-topic]] is used because it is not one of
the audit categories nor a criterion that excludes a record without
judging the work, so the report of step~11 counts it plainly as not
bearing.

The pattern accepts the options in any order, with a space or an
equals sign, and either [[--paper-id]] or [[--doi]]; the anchor is
Scholar's summary line.
The step cannot check that every paper of the tier was decided, and
does not need to: a paper left pending stays in the tier and out of
the rest, which the next step's counts show.

<<decide the tier step>>=
# Screen the tier

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+decide\s+["']?tiers["']?(?=\s|$)(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--source[\s=]+human(?=\s|$))(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--(?:paper-id|doi)[\s=]+\S)
  - mode: regex
    pattern: marked\s+(?:kept|discarded)\s+in\s+session
hint: "Read the ids with cut -f1,2 tier1.tsv, then decide with --source human, --keep or --discard --tag off-topic, and --paper-id for each paper."
```

Read the first tier's sheet, then decide every paper on it: keep the
ones about literate programming, discard the others with a tag saying
why.

```
cut -f1,2 tier1.tsv
scholar sessions decide tiers --source human --keep --paper-id <id> --paper-id <id>
scholar sessions decide tiers --source human --discard --tag off-topic --paper-id <id>
```

Replace each `<id>` with an id from the first column (`doi:...` or
`hash:...`); give `--paper-id` once per paper.
@

\section{Step 9: Draw a sample of the rest}

The papers the tier leaves out are not screened, and the method has
to say something about them all the same.
[[--rest-sample 2 --seed 7]] draws two of them at random, with the same
tier option as before, and records the draw in the session (the
export saves it, after keeping a copy of the file).
The seed makes the draw repeatable: the draw keys the sorted ids of the
rest with Python's [[random.Random(seed)]] and takes the smallest keys
([[smallest_keys]] in the LLM review module), so the same seed over the
same pending papers draws the same ones.

The step makes the third critical aspect visible: the tier line now
says \enquote{0 of M pending papers selected}, because the tier is
recomputed from the papers still pending and step~8 decided the whole
tier, while the rest is what it was before step~8: the pending papers
outside the tier.
When the rest holds no more than two papers, the draw takes all of them
and says \enquote{(a census)}; the instructions name that case, since
it depends on the day's answers.
The anchors are the draw's line and the confirmation that it is
recorded, both fixed text.

<<draw the rest step>>=
# Draw a sample of the rest

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+export\s+["']?tiers["']?(?=\s|$)(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--top[\s=]+3(?=\s|$))(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--rest-sample[\s=]+2(?=\s|$))(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--seed[\s=]+7(?=\s|$))
  - Sample of the rest
  - The draw is recorded in the session.
hint: "The tier export again, with --top 3, --rest-sample 2 --seed 7 and the base name rest."
```

The papers outside the tier are not screened. Draw a random sample of
two of them instead, and look at the sheet it writes:

```
scholar sessions export tiers -f screen-sheet --pending --top 3 --rest-sample 2 --seed 7 -o rest
cut -f1,2 rest.tsv
```

Look at the `Tier:` line first: it now selects no pending paper, since
you decided the whole tier. The tier and the rest are computed from the
papers that are still pending, so deciding the tier did not change the
rest: it is still the pending papers outside the tier.

The draw is recorded in the session under the id printed after `Sample
of the rest`, and the seed makes it repeatable: the same seed over the
same pending papers draws the same papers. If the rest holds no more
than two papers, all of them are taken and the line says `(a census)`.
@

\section{Step 10: The report before the sample is screened}

The fourth critical aspect is that the rest is estimated, and that an
estimate needs its sample screened.
The learner asks for the report before screening the sample, so the
first sentence they see about the rest is Scholar's refusal to give a
rate, with its reason.
This half of the contrast comes first on purpose: a learner who saw the
rate first would read the refusal as an error; seen first, it is the
state every draw starts in.

The anchor is the fixed opening of the reason.

<<report before screening step>>=
# The report before the sample is screened

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+tier-sample\s+["']?tiers["']?(?=\s|$)(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--report(?=\s|$))
  - "No rate is given:"
hint: "Ask sessions tier-sample for the report on the session tiers."
```

Before you screen the sample, ask for the report on it:

```
scholar sessions tier-sample tiers --report
```

The sentence names the tier's rule in words and says how many records
the rest held, then gives no rate, and says why.
@

\section{Step 11: Screen the sample and report again}

The second half of the contrast: the same report after the drawn papers
are decided, nothing else changed.
The sentence now gives how many of the drawn records bore on the
question and the share; for a sample, also its 95\,\% modified Wilson
interval, the interval [[llm sample --report]] uses
(\cref{sec:llm-sample-interval} explains the choice), while a census
of the rest gets its exact count and no interval.
With two records the interval is wide, and the instructions make the
learner look at its upper end, since that is the part a method section
must not drop: a sample in which none bore does not show that none of
the rest does, and Scholar's sentence says so in that case.

The anchors are the decide summary and the text that follows the count
only when a rate is given, [[bore on the question (]]: the refusal of
step~10 also says \enquote{bore on the question}, but never followed by
a parenthesis.

<<screen the sample step>>=
# Screen the sample and report again

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+decide\s+["']?tiers["']?(?=\s|$)(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--source[\s=]+human(?=\s|$))(?=(?:.|\\\n)*\s--(?:paper-id|doi)[\s=]+\S)
  - mode: regex
    pattern: marked\s+(?:kept|discarded)\s+in\s+session
  - mode: regex
    pattern: bore\s+on\s+the\s+question\s+\(
hint: "Decide the papers of rest.tsv with --source human, then run sessions tier-sample tiers --report again."
```

Decide the drawn papers the way you decided the tier, then ask for the
report again:

```
scholar sessions decide tiers --source human --keep --paper-id <id>
scholar sessions decide tiers --source human --discard --tag off-topic --paper-id <id>
scholar sessions tier-sample tiers --report
```

The sentence now says how many of the drawn records bore on the
question (a keep counts as bearing here, since the session uses no
categories) and the share, with a 95% interval for the share among all
the records the tier left out. Look at the upper end of the interval:
with two records it is wide, and if none bore, the sentence adds that
this does not show that none of the rest does. If the draw took the
whole rest (a census), the count is exact and there is no interval.
@

\section{Step 12: What a method section can claim}

The closing question is the fusion move: it asks the learner to hold
the tier, the rest and the sample together and judge statements a
method section might make.
Each wrong option is the conclusion of a learner who missed one
critical aspect: \enquote{every record was screened} forgets that the
rest was not; \enquote{none of the rest bear} reads a sample with no
bearing record as an empty rest; \enquote{the first three of every
answer, whatever its order} forgets step~7.
The right options are the two the lesson has shown Scholar itself
stating: the rule of the tier in words, and the share of the screened
sample, or of the whole rest when the draw was a census.

<<what can be claimed question step>>=
# What can a method section claim?

```tutorial-step
kind: multi_select
options:
  - Every record the searches returned was screened.
  - The first tier held the records at place 3 or better in an answer ordered by relevance.
  - The records outside the tier were screened by a random sample (or all of them, when they were no more than the sample's size), and the share of those screened that bore on the question is given.
  - If no sampled record bore on the question, none of the records outside the tier does.
  - The first tier held the first three records of every answer, whatever its order.
answers:
  - The first tier held the records at place 3 or better in an answer ordered by relevance.
  - The records outside the tier were screened by a random sample (or all of them, when they were no more than the sample's size), and the share of those screened that bore on the question is given.
hint: "Compare each statement with the sentence sessions tier-sample --report printed, and with the tier's counts."
```

Which of these statements does what you did in this lesson support?

The report's sentence is written for a method section: it names the
rule that selected the tier, the date and size of the rest, the seed,
who screened the sample, and the share, with its interval when the draw
was a sample.
